-- Q1: medium / L2 — Excess IDC as preference (65% net income test)
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Garza invested $300,000 in an oil and gas partnership during 2026. The partnership elected to expense $250,000 in intangible drilling costs (IDCs) under Section 263(c). The amortized amount over 10 years would be $25,000. Under what circumstances do the excess IDCs become a tax preference item for AMT under Section 57?', '["Excess IDCs are always a preference item regardless of net income from the property", "Excess IDCs are a preference item when they exceed 65% of the taxpayer''s net income from the oil and gas property", "Excess IDCs are a preference item only when total IDCs exceed $100,000 in a single tax year", "Excess IDCs are never a preference item for individual taxpayers investing through partnerships"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(2), excess intangible drilling costs are a tax preference item when they exceed 65% of the taxpayer''s net income from the oil and gas property. The excess is the amount by which IDCs expensed exceed what would have been deductible using 10-year amortization. Unlike choice A suggests, the preference applies only when the 65% threshold is exceeded, not automatically on all IDCs.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — AMT included in estimated tax safe harbor base
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Morrison, a single filer, paid $85,000 in regular tax and $15,000 in AMT for 2025, totaling $100,000. For 2026, her AGI exceeds $150,000. She expects no AMT liability in 2026 with an estimated regular tax of $90,000. Analyze which safe harbor amount Morrison should use for 2026 estimated payments to avoid the underpayment penalty.', '["$85,000, which is 100% of the prior year regular tax excluding AMT", "$93,500, which is 110% of the prior year regular tax excluding AMT", "$110,000, which is 110% of the prior year total tax including AMT", "$99,000, which is 110% of the prior year total tax less the AMT portion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6654, the estimated tax safe harbor is based on the prior year''s total tax liability, which includes AMT. Since Morrison''s prior year AGI exceeded $150,000, the safe harbor is 110% of prior year total tax: $100,000 x 110% = $110,000. Choice A incorrectly excludes AMT from the prior year base and uses 100% instead of the required 110% for high-income taxpayers.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Percentage depletion excess over adjusted basis
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Thornton owns a working interest in a gold mine. For 2026, his percentage depletion deduction under Section 613 is $80,000, while the property''s adjusted basis at year-end is $55,000. Cost depletion would have been $30,000. Analyze the AMT tax preference item arising from Thornton''s depletion deduction.', '["$50,000, the excess of percentage depletion over cost depletion", "$80,000, the total percentage depletion claimed", "$30,000, equal to the cost depletion amount", "$25,000, the excess of percentage depletion over the property''s adjusted basis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(1), the tax preference for depletion is the excess of the percentage depletion deduction over the adjusted basis of the property at year-end. Here, $80,000 percentage depletion minus $55,000 adjusted basis equals $25,000. Choice A incorrectly compares percentage depletion to cost depletion rather than to the property''s adjusted basis, which is the statutory measure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — MTC only from deferral items, not exclusion items
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Kendrick paid $22,000 in AMT for 2025. Of this amount, $16,000 was attributable to the exercise of incentive stock options (a timing/deferral item) and $6,000 was attributable to private activity bond interest (an exclusion item). Under IRC Section 53, evaluate how much minimum tax credit Kendrick can carry forward to future years.', '["$16,000, because only AMT attributable to deferral items generates a minimum tax credit", "$22,000, because all AMT paid generates a minimum tax credit regardless of source", "$6,000, because the credit applies only to exclusion items to prevent double taxation", "$0, because the minimum tax credit was repealed for individual taxpayers by the TCJA"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 53(d), the minimum tax credit is limited to AMT attributable to deferral (timing) items, such as the ISO exercise spread or depreciation differences. Exclusion preferences like private activity bond interest do not generate MTC because the income is permanently excluded, not merely deferred. Choice B incorrectly treats all AMT as generating a credit, ignoring the deferral-only limitation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Section 56 defines AMT adjustments
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Which IRC section specifies the adjustments used to convert regular taxable income into alternative minimum taxable income?', '["Section 55", "Section 56", "Section 57", "Section 58"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IRC Section 56 enumerates the specific adjustments applied to taxable income when computing AMTI. These adjustments can either increase or decrease AMTI (e.g., SALT add-back, depreciation timing differences). Section 55 defines the AMT itself, while Section 57 defines preference items, which are distinct from adjustments because preferences always increase AMTI.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Mutual fund PAB interest pass-through
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Franklin invests in a municipal bond mutual fund that holds both governmental and private activity bonds. In 2026, the fund distributes $12,000 in tax-exempt interest, of which $4,500 is attributable to private activity bonds. The fund reports this on Form 1099-INT. How is Franklin''s AMT affected by these distributions?', '["The entire $12,000 is an AMT preference item because all municipal bond interest triggers AMT", "No AMT preference arises because mutual fund distributions are not subject to AMT", "The $7,500 from governmental bonds is the AMT preference item", "The $4,500 attributable to private activity bonds is an AMT preference item"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(5), tax-exempt interest from specified private activity bonds is an AMT preference item, regardless of whether received directly or through a mutual fund pass-through. The $4,500 PAB interest must be added to AMTI. Choice A is incorrect because governmental bond interest under Section 103 is fully exempt for both regular tax and AMT — only PAB interest triggers the preference.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — QSBS Section 1202 post-2010 AMT treatment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Webb sold qualified small business stock (QSBS) acquired in 2015, realizing a $500,000 gain. She qualifies for the 100% exclusion under Section 1202(a). Under pre-2010 rules, 7% of excluded gain was an AMT preference under Section 57(a)(7). For Webb''s 2026 sale of post-2010 QSBS, which statement is correct?', '["No AMT preference arises because the 7% rule does not apply to QSBS acquired after September 27, 2010", "$35,000 is an AMT preference, computed as 7% of the $500,000 excluded gain", "The entire $500,000 excluded gain is an AMT preference item under Section 57(a)(7)", "$250,000 is an AMT preference, computed as 50% of the excluded gain"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1202(a)(4), for QSBS acquired after September 27, 2010, the 100% gain exclusion applies for both regular tax and AMT purposes — no portion is treated as a preference item. The 7% AMT preference under Section 57(a)(7) applied only to pre-2010 acquisitions with 50% or 75% exclusions. Choice B incorrectly applies the obsolete 7% rule to post-2010 stock.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Trust/estate AMT exemption dollar amount
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'What is the approximate AMT exemption amount for estates and trusts in 2026?', '["$29,900", "$68,500", "$88,100", "$137,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(d)(1)(D), estates and trusts receive an AMT exemption of approximately $29,900 for 2026 (indexed for inflation). This is significantly lower than individual exemptions. Choice C ($88,100) is the single filer exemption, choice D ($137,000) is the MFJ exemption, and choice B ($68,500) is the MFS exemption.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — MFS vs MFJ combined exemption comparison
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Ingram and Nguyen are married with combined AMTI of $800,000. Filing jointly, the MFJ exemption is $137,000 with phase-out beginning at $1,156,300. Filing separately, each reports $400,000 AMTI with an MFS exemption of $68,500 and phase-out beginning at $578,150. Analyze the combined AMT exemption under each filing status.', '["$137,000 under MFJ and $0 under MFS because separate filers lose the entire exemption", "$137,000 under MFJ and $68,500 under MFS because only one spouse claims the exemption", "$137,000 under MFJ and $137,000 under MFS because neither status triggers a phase-out at this income level", "$68,500 under MFJ and $137,000 under MFS because separate filing doubles the exemption"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 55(d), the MFJ exemption is $137,000 with phase-out at $1,156,300 — no reduction at $800,000 combined AMTI. Filing separately, each spouse gets $68,500 with phase-out at $578,150. Since each spouse''s $400,000 AMTI is below $578,150, neither triggers the phase-out, so combined MFS exemption is also $137,000. Choice B incorrectly assumes only one spouse claims the exemption.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Corporate CAMT under IRA calculation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Apex Industries has average annual adjusted financial statement income (AFSI) of $1.2 billion over the past three years and regular corporate tax liability of $180 million for 2026. Its AFSI for 2026 is $1.5 billion, and its tentative CAMT (before credits) is $225 million. Evaluate Apex''s corporate alternative minimum tax liability under the Inflation Reduction Act.', '["$225 million, because the CAMT replaces the regular corporate tax for applicable corporations", "$0, because the CAMT does not apply until AFSI exceeds $2 billion", "$45 million, the excess of the tentative CAMT of $225 million over the regular tax of $180 million", "$180 million, because the 15% CAMT rate produces a lower liability than the 21% regular rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 55(a) as amended by the Inflation Reduction Act, the CAMT applies to corporations with average AFSI exceeding $1 billion. The CAMT equals the excess of tentative minimum tax (15% of adjusted AFSI) over regular tax: $225 million minus $180 million equals $45 million. Choice A incorrectly treats CAMT as replacing regular tax rather than supplementing it when the 15% book minimum exceeds regular tax.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Nonrefundable credits + MTC ordering with TMT floor
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Blake, a single filer, has regular tax liability of $62,000 and tentative minimum tax of $52,000 for 2026. She claims $6,000 in nonrefundable personal credits and has an $8,000 minimum tax credit carryforward from prior ISO exercises. Evaluate Blake''s total tax liability and remaining MTC carryforward.', '["$48,000 total tax with $0 MTC remaining, because both credits fully offset without limitation", "$56,000 total tax with $8,000 MTC remaining, because MTC cannot be used when personal credits are also claimed", "$52,000 total tax with $0 MTC remaining, because MTC fully offsets the difference between regular tax and TMT", "$52,000 total tax with $4,000 MTC remaining, because only $4,000 of MTC can be used against the excess over TMT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Sections 26(a) and 53, personal credits reduce regular tax from $62,000 to $56,000. The MTC is limited to the excess of regular tax (after other credits) over TMT: $56,000 minus $52,000 equals $4,000. Only $4,000 of the $8,000 MTC is used, leaving $4,000 to carry forward. Total tax is $52,000. Choice A incorrectly applies the full $14,000 in credits without recognizing the TMT floor limitation on MTC.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L2 — Governmental bond interest NOT a preference
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Lawson holds two types of municipal bonds in 2026: $100,000 in general obligation bonds issued by the State of Oregon and $50,000 in qualified private activity bonds. The state bonds pay $4,000 in interest and the PABs pay $2,500. Which interest amount creates an AMT preference item?', '["$4,000 from the state general obligation bonds", "$2,500 from the qualified private activity bonds", "$6,500, the combined total of all municipal bond interest", "Neither amount, because all municipal bond interest is exempt from AMT"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(5), only interest from specified private activity bonds is an AMT preference item. Interest from governmental bonds — such as general obligation bonds — is fully exempt for both regular tax and AMT. Choice D incorrectly states that all municipal bond interest avoids AMT, when in fact PAB interest must be added to AMTI.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Trust AMT exemption phase-out calculation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'The Sterling Family Trust has AMTI of $160,350 before the exemption for 2026. The trust AMT exemption is $29,900, and the phase-out begins at $100,350 at a rate of 25 cents per dollar. Calculate the trust''s reduced AMT exemption.', '["$14,900, after reducing the exemption by 25% of AMTI exceeding $100,350", "$29,900, because the phase-out does not apply until AMTI exceeds $200,000 for trusts", "$0, because the trust''s AMTI fully eliminates the exemption at this income level", "$22,425, after reducing the exemption by 25% of AMTI exceeding $130,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The phase-out reduces the exemption by 25% of AMTI above $100,350: ($160,350 minus $100,350) times 25% equals $15,000. The reduced exemption is $29,900 minus $15,000 equals $14,900. Under IRC Section 55(d)(3), the exemption is fully eliminated when AMTI reaches $220,150 ($100,350 plus $119,600). Choice C incorrectly treats the exemption as fully phased out at this income level.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Real property depreciation — no AMT adjustment post-1998
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Kowalski placed a commercial building in service in 2020 and claims straight-line depreciation over 39 years for regular tax purposes. For AMT purposes, analyze the depreciation adjustment required under Section 56(a)(1) for real property placed in service after 1998.', '["The building must be depreciated over 40 years using the 150% declining balance method for AMT", "The building must be depreciated over 50 years using the straight-line method for AMT", "The AMT adjustment is 20% of the regular tax depreciation claimed each year", "No AMT adjustment is required because straight-line depreciation is used for both regular tax and AMT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 56(a)(1), real property placed in service after 1998 uses the same straight-line method for both regular tax and AMT purposes, so no adjustment is required. The AMT depreciation adjustment for real property primarily affected pre-1999 assets where different methods or periods were used. Choice A incorrectly applies an accelerated AMT method to real property, which is not required under current law.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Individual ATNOL 80% limitation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Fontaine, a single filer, has AMTI of $600,000 before applying the alternative tax net operating loss (ATNOL) deduction for 2026. She has an ATNOL carryforward of $500,000 from tax years after 2017. Under IRC Section 56(d), the ATNOL deduction is limited to 80% of AMTI. Evaluate Fontaine''s AMTI after the ATNOL and her remaining carryforward.', '["$100,000 AMTI with $0 remaining, because the full $500,000 ATNOL is deducted", "$600,000 AMTI with $500,000 remaining, because ATNOL deductions were eliminated by the TCJA", "$120,000 AMTI with $20,000 remaining, because the ATNOL is limited to 80% of AMTI", "$0 AMTI with $0 remaining, because the ATNOL fully offsets AMTI with no percentage cap"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 56(d), the alternative tax NOL deduction mirrors the regular tax NOL limitation — for NOLs arising after 2017, the deduction is limited to 80% of AMTI. Fontaine can deduct 80% times $600,000 equals $480,000, leaving $120,000 AMTI and $20,000 unused ATNOL to carry forward indefinitely. Choice A incorrectly allows the full $500,000 deduction without the 80% cap.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Section 57 defines tax preference items
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under the AMT system, what does IRC Section 57 specifically define?', '["The adjustments to taxable income for computing AMTI", "The items of tax preference that are always added to taxable income for AMT", "The AMT exemption amounts and phase-out thresholds", "The minimum tax credit carryforward rules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IRC Section 57 enumerates the items of tax preference — such as private activity bond interest, excess intangible drilling costs, and excess percentage depletion — that are always added back to taxable income when computing AMTI. Section 56 covers adjustments (which can increase or decrease AMTI), Section 55(d) covers exemption amounts, and Section 53 covers the minimum tax credit.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Who is subject to AMT (broad applicability)
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under IRC Section 55, which categories of taxpayers may be subject to the alternative minimum tax?', '["Only C corporations with gross receipts exceeding $10 million", "Only individual taxpayers with AGI above $500,000", "Only trusts and estates with distributable net income above $100,000", "Individuals, applicable corporations, trusts, and estates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 55(a), the AMT applies broadly to noncorporate taxpayers (individuals, trusts, and estates) and, under the Inflation Reduction Act, to applicable corporations meeting the $1 billion average AFSI threshold. There is no AGI floor for individual AMT — liability depends on the TMT exceeding regular tax. Choice B incorrectly implies a $500,000 AGI threshold that does not exist in the statute.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L3 — Kiddie tax and AMT interaction
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Parker, age 15, has $40,000 in unearned income from a trust and $5,000 in earned income for 2026. The kiddie tax applies, taxing unearned income above approximately $2,500 at the parent''s marginal rate. Parker also holds private activity bonds generating $8,000 in tax-exempt interest. Analyze the AMT implications for Parker''s return.', '["Parker must file Form 6251 because the $8,000 PAB interest is an AMT preference item added to Parker''s own AMTI, separate from the kiddie tax computation", "No AMT applies because the kiddie tax already taxes Parker at the parent''s higher rate, eliminating AMT exposure", "The $8,000 PAB interest is reported on the parent''s return as part of the kiddie tax calculation", "Parker''s AMT is computed using the parent''s AMT exemption amount rather than the child''s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Sections 55 and 59(j), a child subject to the kiddie tax computes AMT on the child''s own return using the child''s own exemption and preference items. The $8,000 PAB interest is Parker''s AMT preference and must be reported on Parker''s Form 6251. Choice B incorrectly assumes that the kiddie tax rate substitution eliminates AMT exposure, but AMT is a separate parallel computation regardless of how regular tax rates are applied.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Multi-year MTC recovery across years
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Douglas paid $30,000 in AMT in Year 1 entirely due to ISO exercises (a deferral item). In Year 2, with no ISO activity, his regular tax after other credits is $75,000 and TMT is $50,000. In Year 3, his regular tax after credits is $60,000 and TMT is $55,000. Evaluate Douglas''s MTC usage over Years 2 and 3.', '["$25,000 in Year 2 and $0 in Year 3, with the remaining $5,000 expiring after one carryforward year", "$25,000 in Year 2 and $5,000 in Year 3, fully recovering the $30,000 MTC over two years", "$30,000 in Year 2, using the full credit whenever regular tax exceeds TMT by any amount", "$0 in both years, because MTC from ISO exercises requires selling the underlying shares first"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 53, the MTC is limited each year to the excess of regular tax (after other credits) over TMT. In Year 2, the limit is $75,000 minus $50,000 equals $25,000. In Year 3, it is $60,000 minus $55,000 equals $5,000. The combined $30,000 is fully recovered. Choice C incorrectly allows the full $30,000 in Year 2 despite the $25,000 statutory cap. The MTC carries forward indefinitely under Section 53(b), so choice A''s expiration claim is wrong.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Section 174 research costs — TCJA eliminated AMT adjustment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Calloway Inc. spent $200,000 on qualified research and experimental expenditures in 2026. Under Section 174 as amended by the TCJA, these costs must be capitalized and amortized over 5 years for domestic research for regular tax purposes. Analyze whether an AMT adjustment is required for these expenditures.', '["A positive AMT adjustment is required because AMT requires 10-year amortization of research costs", "A negative AMT adjustment is required because AMT allows immediate expensing of research costs", "An AMT adjustment equal to 50% of the expenditure is required under Section 56(b)(2)", "No AMT adjustment is required because the TCJA''s mandatory capitalization eliminated the prior-law difference between regular tax and AMT treatment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Before the TCJA, taxpayers could expense Section 174 costs for regular tax but were required to capitalize them over 10 years for AMT under Section 56(b)(2)(A), creating an adjustment. The TCJA''s mandatory 5-year capitalization for regular tax eliminated this discrepancy — both systems now require capitalization. Choice A applies the pre-TCJA AMT rule that is no longer relevant under current law.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — AMT calculation starts with regular taxable income
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'What is the starting point for calculating alternative minimum taxable income for an individual taxpayer?', '["Adjusted gross income", "Gross income", "Regular taxable income", "Total income before exemptions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 55(b)(2), AMTI begins with regular taxable income, to which AMT adjustments (Section 56) and tax preference items (Section 57) are added or subtracted. Choice A is incorrect because AGI is an intermediate figure in the regular tax computation; the AMT calculation starts after all regular deductions have been applied to arrive at taxable income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — AMT exemption function (reduces base, not a credit)
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'How does the AMT exemption function in the alternative minimum tax calculation?', '["It reduces AMTI before AMT rates are applied, similar to a flat deduction from the tax base", "It is a dollar-for-dollar credit applied against the tentative minimum tax", "It eliminates the first $88,100 of tax preference items for single filers", "It replaces the standard deduction in the AMT computation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(b)(1), the AMT exemption is subtracted from AMTI to determine the base on which AMT rates are applied — functioning as a flat reduction of the taxable base. Choice B incorrectly characterizes it as a credit against tax rather than a base reduction. Choice D is wrong because the standard deduction is an AMT adjustment added back to AMTI; the AMT exemption is a separate and distinct concept.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Section 59(e) election eliminates IDC preference
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Mitchell elected to amortize $180,000 in intangible drilling costs over 60 months under IRC Section 59(e) rather than expensing them immediately. For AMT purposes, which statement correctly describes the effect of Mitchell''s election?', '["The election has no AMT impact because IDCs are always a preference item regardless of amortization method", "The election increases the AMT preference because amortization creates a timing difference", "The election eliminates the IDC tax preference item because the same deduction is used for both regular tax and AMT", "The election converts the IDC from a preference item to an AMT adjustment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 59(e), a taxpayer may elect to amortize certain preference items — including intangible drilling costs — over the applicable period (60 months for IDCs). This election eliminates the AMT preference because the same amortized deduction is claimed for both regular tax and AMT, removing the disparity. Choice A incorrectly states that IDCs are always a preference regardless of the taxpayer''s method election.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Personal property depreciation 200% to 150% DB for AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Russo placed 7-year MACRS personal property in service in 2026 and elected out of bonus depreciation. For regular tax, she uses the 200% declining balance method. Under IRC Section 56(a)(1), what depreciation method must she use for AMT purposes on this property?', '["Straight-line method over the ADS recovery period", "150% declining balance method over the regular MACRS recovery period", "200% declining balance method, the same as regular tax", "Units-of-production method based on actual usage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 56(a)(1)(A), personal property depreciated using the 200% declining balance method for regular tax must use the 150% declining balance method for AMT, creating a timing adjustment. Choice C incorrectly states the same method applies for both systems. Choice A applies the ADS straight-line method, which is not the default AMT requirement for personal property.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Section 179 — no AMT adjustment required
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Henderson, a small business owner, elected to expense $200,000 of qualifying equipment under Section 179 in 2026 for regular tax. She has sufficient business income to absorb the full deduction. Analyze the AMT treatment of Henderson''s Section 179 deduction.', '["No AMT adjustment is required because the Section 179 deduction is allowed identically for both regular tax and AMT", "The $200,000 must be capitalized and depreciated over 5 years for AMT, creating a $160,000 positive adjustment", "50% of the Section 179 deduction is an AMT preference item under Section 57", "The Section 179 deduction is disallowed entirely for AMT, creating a $200,000 add-back"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 56, the Section 179 immediate expensing election is allowed for both regular tax and AMT purposes without adjustment. Unlike the 200% declining balance method that requires a 150% declining balance adjustment for AMT, Section 179 creates no AMT disparity. Choice D incorrectly treats Section 179 like the standard deduction, which is fully added back for AMT.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Capital loss $3,000 annual limit same for AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'For 2026, Yamamoto has $20,000 in net long-term capital losses and $250,000 in ordinary income. For regular tax, the capital loss deduction is limited to $3,000 under Section 1211(b). Analyze how this capital loss limitation is treated for AMT purposes.', '["The $3,000 capital loss limit is doubled to $6,000 for AMT", "Capital losses are not deductible at all for AMT purposes", "The full $20,000 net capital loss is deductible against AMTI", "The same $3,000 annual limit applies for AMT with no adjustment required"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 56, the $3,000 annual net capital loss limitation from Section 1211(b) applies identically for both regular tax and AMT. No AMT adjustment is required. The $17,000 excess carries forward under the same rules for both systems. Choice A incorrectly suggests AMT provides a more generous capital loss deduction, and choice B incorrectly disallows capital losses entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Child tax credit can offset AMT post-TCJA
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'For 2026, a taxpayer owes $5,000 in AMT in addition to $40,000 in regular tax. The taxpayer has $4,000 in child tax credits available. Under current law as amended by the TCJA, analyze whether the child tax credit can offset the AMT.', '["The child tax credit cannot offset any AMT — it only reduces regular tax liability", "The child tax credit can offset AMT but only up to 50% of the AMT amount", "The child tax credit offsets regular tax first, and any remaining credit offsets AMT dollar-for-dollar", "The child tax credit can offset both regular tax and AMT without limitation under current law"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 26(a) as amended by the TCJA, nonrefundable personal credits including the child tax credit can offset both regular tax and AMT without limitation. Before the TCJA, personal credits generally could not reduce total tax below the TMT. Choice A reflects the pre-2018 rule that is no longer in effect under current law.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Corporate CAMT rate (15%)
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under the Inflation Reduction Act, what tax rate applies to the Corporate Alternative Minimum Tax for applicable corporations?', '["21%", "15%", "28%", "10%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 55(b)(2)(A) as enacted by the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022, the corporate alternative minimum tax applies a 15% rate to the adjusted financial statement income of applicable corporations with average AFSI exceeding $1 billion. Choice A (21%) is the regular corporate income tax rate, and choice C (28%) is the top individual AMT rate, not the corporate CAMT rate.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — MFS AMT exemption dollar amount
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'What is the approximate AMT exemption amount for a married taxpayer filing separately in 2026?', '["$68,500", "$88,100", "$137,000", "$29,900"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(d)(1)(B), a married individual filing separately receives an AMT exemption of approximately $68,500 for 2026, which is exactly half of the MFJ exemption of $137,000. Choice B ($88,100) is the exemption for single or head-of-household filers, and choice D ($29,900) applies to estates and trusts.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — 501(c)(3) bond exception from PAB preference
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Graham holds municipal bonds issued by a local hospital that qualifies as a 501(c)(3) organization. The bonds pay $6,000 in annual interest. For AMT purposes, classify the interest from Graham''s bonds.', '["The $6,000 is a tax preference item because hospital bonds are private activity bonds", "The $6,000 is partially excluded — only 50% is an AMT preference item", "The $6,000 is not an AMT preference item because qualified 501(c)(3) bonds are specifically excluded from the PAB preference", "The $6,000 is an AMT adjustment rather than a preference item"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(5)(C)(iii), interest on qualified 501(c)(3) bonds is specifically excluded from the private activity bond AMT preference. Although 501(c)(3) bonds are technically a type of private activity bond, Congress carved out an exemption to avoid penalizing charitable and educational institutions. Choice A incorrectly treats all private activity bonds identically without recognizing the 501(c)(3) exception.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
